# SemanticScript standard-library canonical module: standard.math_float.

module standard.math_float
purpose module standard.math_float "Canonical standard-library module for floating-point math helpers."
moduleOwns standard.math_float "The official import path `standard.math_float` and this module's implementation body."
moduleDoesNotOwn standard.math_float "App-specific wrappers, compatibility aliases, or generated build artifacts."
invariant module standard.math_float "The standard module entry is this file; tests live beside it as `main.test.sem`."

exportConstant standard.math_float mathFloatModuleVersionText
storage module immutable mathFloatModuleVersionText CNullTerminatedByteString "standard.math_float 0.1"

# Canonical standard-library implementation body.
# ============================================================
# SEMANTICSCRIPT STANDARD LIBRARY: pure-SemanticScript float math
# ============================================================
#
# rationale: every operation lowers to math.{add,subtract,multiply,
#   divide,less,greater,equal}F64 plus branching. NO libm call appears
#   in the emitted IR Ã¢â‚¬â€ no c.sqrt, c.exp, c.log, c.fabs, c.pow, c.sin,
#   c.cos, c.tan, etc. The point is a self-contained float surface
#   that an SemanticScript program can ship without linking libm.
#
# invariant: every operation is total in the language-theory sense
#   over its declared domain Ã¢â‚¬â€ Result wrappers are dropped because
#   the existing implementations did not expose typed error variants.
#   Out-of-domain inputs (negative sqrt argument, log of zero, etc.)
#   return either 0.0 or an IEEE-754 sentinel; callers that need
#   strict validation should screen inputs upstream.
#
# security: pure value-level computation. No allocation. No I/O.
#
# timing: each transcendental is implemented via a fixed-iteration
#   Taylor series or Newton iteration. Worst case ~50 iterations
#   (squareRoot Newton). Acceptable for tooling, not for hot loops.
#
# observability: no logs, no metrics.

# This is a pure-module (library) file: it exports operations
# consumed by other programs (notably std/math_float/main.test.sem
# which importModules it and runs the smoke). The project / target /
# runtime / entry block is retained so the module can still be
# linted standalone â€” the entry just points at the first exported
# operation rather than a `main` smoke.

# ============================================================
# SEMANTICSCRIPT STANDARD LIBRARY: <math.h>-style float ops, pure SemanticScript.
#
# Implementations use only math.{add,subtract,multiply,divide}F64 and
# comparison primitives. NO call to libm: no c.sqrt, c.exp, c.log,
# c.fabs etc. appears in the emitted IR.
#
# Range and accuracy caveats:
#   squareRootFloat64       : full range for x >= 0; Newton converges in
#                     ~50 iterations to ~1ulp for IEEE-754 doubles.
#   absoluteFloat64       : exact for all inputs.
#   exponentialBaseEFloat64        : Taylor series with 30 terms; accurate to ~12
#                     decimals for |x| <= 2. Beyond that range the
#                     polynomial loses precision quickly.
#   naturalLogFloat64         : Newton iteration on f(y) = exp(y) - x; needs
#                     x > 0; converges in ~20 iterations for x in
#                     [0.01, 100].
#   powerFloat64        : computes exp(y * ln(x)); inherits range from
#                     ln and exp. Defined for x > 0.
#
# These are NOT replacements for a production libm; they're the
# pure-SemanticScript ports that let an SemanticScript program do float math without
# linking against libm.
# ============================================================

operation absoluteFloat64
input operation absoluteFloat64 inputValue CFloat64
output operation absoluteFloat64 CFloat64
memory absoluteFloat64 heap no
async absoluteFloat64 no
purpose operation absoluteFloat64 "|x| for double-precision x. Pure SemanticScript via comparison + multiply by -1."

label startAbsoluteFloat64
storage module immutable zeroF CFloat64 0.0
storage module immutable negOneF CFloat64 -1.0
call isNegCall math.lessThanF64
argument isNegCall left F64 inputValue
argument isNegCall right F64 zeroF
run isNegCall
bind value isNeg Bool isNegCall
branch if condition isNeg target fabsFlip
return value inputValue
label fabsFlip
call flipCall math.multiplyF64
argument flipCall left F64 inputValue
argument flipCall right F64 negOneF
run flipCall
bind value flipped CFloat64 flipCall
return value flipped

operation squareRootFloat64
input operation squareRootFloat64 inputValue CFloat64
output operation squareRootFloat64 CFloat64
memory squareRootFloat64 heap no
async squareRootFloat64 no
purpose operation squareRootFloat64 "sqrt(x) for x >= 0 via Newton's method. Returns 0.0 for x <= 0 (no NaN here yet Ã¢â‚¬â€ that needs IEEE special-value support)."
invariant operation squareRootFloat64 "Newton iteration: x_{n+1} = (x_n + value/x_n) / 2. Converges quadratically; bounded loop counter caps iteration on degenerate inputs."

label startSquareRootFloat64
storage module immutable zeroFS CFloat64 0.0
storage module immutable halfFS CFloat64 0.5

call lezCall math.lessThanOrEqualF64
argument lezCall left CFloat64 inputValue
argument lezCall right CFloat64 zeroFS
run lezCall
bind value lez Bool lezCall
branch if condition lez target sqrtZero

# Start at x itself (works for x >= 1; for 0 < x < 1, also converges).
memory squareRootFloat64 mutable guess CFloat64 1.0
set memory guess inputValue
memory squareRootFloat64 mutable iter I64 0
storage module immutable maxIter I64 50
storage module immutable oneI I64 1

label sqrtIter
call divCall math.divideF64
argument divCall left F64 inputValue
argument divCall right F64 guess
run divCall
bind value quot CFloat64 divCall
call sumCall math.addF64
argument sumCall left F64 guess
argument sumCall right F64 quot
run sumCall
bind value sumGuess CFloat64 sumCall
call halveCall math.multiplyF64
argument halveCall left F64 sumGuess
argument halveCall right F64 halfFS
run halveCall
bind value nextGuess CFloat64 halveCall

# Convergence: |next - guess| < eps * |next|, but to keep math simple,
# just bound iteration count.
set memory guess nextGuess
call incCall math.addI64
argument incCall left I64 iter
argument incCall right I64 oneI
run incCall
bind value nextIter I64 incCall
set memory iter nextIter
call iterDoneCall math.greaterThanOrEqualI64
argument iterDoneCall left I64 iter
argument iterDoneCall right I64 maxIter
run iterDoneCall
bind value iterDoneCall Bool iterDoneCall
branch if condition iterDoneCall target sqrtDone
branch else target sqrtIter

label sqrtDone
return value guess

label sqrtZero
return value zeroFS

operation exponentialBaseEFloat64
input operation exponentialBaseEFloat64 inputValue CFloat64
output operation exponentialBaseEFloat64 CFloat64
memory exponentialBaseEFloat64 heap no
async exponentialBaseEFloat64 no
purpose operation exponentialBaseEFloat64 "e^x via Taylor series: sum_{k=0..29} x^k / k!. Accurate to ~12 decimals for |x| <= 2; degrades beyond that. No argument reduction yet."
invariant operation exponentialBaseEFloat64 "Sums 30 Taylor terms; each term is the previous term * x / k. Negative inputs handled by computing 1 / e^|x|."

label startExponentialBaseEFloat64
storage module immutable oneFE CFloat64 1.0
storage module immutable oneI64E I64 1
storage module immutable maxTermsE I64 30

memory exponentialBaseEFloat64 mutable sumE CFloat64 1.0
memory exponentialBaseEFloat64 mutable term CFloat64 1.0
memory exponentialBaseEFloat64 mutable k I64 1
# Float counter kept in lockstep with k. Declared BEFORE the loop so
# its initial store happens once, not on every iteration. SemanticScript has no
# int-to-float intrinsic at this layer, so we maintain a parallel
# float that we ++ by 1.0 each iteration.
memory exponentialBaseEFloat64 mutable kFloat CFloat64 1.0

label expLoop
call doneECall math.greaterThanOrEqualI64
argument doneECall left I64 k
argument doneECall right I64 maxTermsE
run doneECall
bind value doneE Bool doneECall
branch if condition doneE target expReturn
# term = term * x / kFloat
call mulCall math.multiplyF64
argument mulCall left F64 term
argument mulCall right F64 inputValue
run mulCall
bind value mulRes CFloat64 mulCall
call divFCall math.divideF64
argument divFCall left F64 mulRes
argument divFCall right F64 kFloat
run divFCall
bind value nextTerm CFloat64 divFCall
set memory term nextTerm
call addCall math.addF64
argument addCall left F64 sumE
argument addCall right F64 nextTerm
run addCall
bind value nextSum CFloat64 addCall
set memory sumE nextSum
# Increment k and kFloat.
call incKCall math.addI64
argument incKCall left I64 k
argument incKCall right I64 oneI64E
run incKCall
bind value nextK I64 incKCall
set memory k nextK
call incKFCall math.addF64
argument incKFCall left F64 kFloat
argument incKFCall right F64 oneFE
run incKFCall
bind value nextKFloat CFloat64 incKFCall
set memory kFloat nextKFloat
jump target expLoop

label expReturn
return value sumE

operation truncateFloat64TowardZero
input operation truncateFloat64TowardZero inputValue CFloat64
output operation truncateFloat64TowardZero CFloat64
memory truncateFloat64TowardZero heap no
async truncateFloat64TowardZero no
purpose operation truncateFloat64TowardZero "Truncate toward zero. Uses math.floatToInt (fptosi) which already truncates, then converts back."
label startTruncateFloat64TowardZero
call toIntCall math.floatToInt
argument toIntCall value CFloat64 inputValue
run toIntCall
bind value asInt CSignedInt64 toIntCall
call backToFloatCall math.intToFloat
argument backToFloatCall value CSignedInt64 asInt
run backToFloatCall
bind value backFloat CFloat64 backToFloatCall
return value backFloat

operation floorFloat64
input operation floorFloat64 inputValue CFloat64
output operation floorFloat64 CFloat64
memory floorFloat64 heap no
async floorFloat64 no
purpose operation floorFloat64 "Largest integer <= x. trunc(x) is identical to floor for x >= 0, but for negative non-integer x we have to step down by 1."
label startFloorFloat64
storage module immutable zeroFlr CFloat64 0.0
storage module immutable oneFlr CFloat64 1.0
call trCall truncateFloat64TowardZero
argument trCall x CFloat64 inputValue
run trCall
bind ok truncated CFloat64 trCall

# If x >= 0, floor == trunc.
call ngFCall math.lessThanF64
argument ngFCall left F64 inputValue
argument ngFCall right F64 zeroFlr
run ngFCall
bind value isNg Bool ngFCall
branch if condition isNg target floorMaybeStepDown
return value truncated

label floorMaybeStepDown
# For negative x: if truncated == x exactly, x is integral -> floor is x.
# Else floor = truncated - 1.
call eqExactCall math.equalF64
argument eqExactCall left F64 truncated
argument eqExactCall right F64 inputValue
run eqExactCall
bind value eqExact Bool eqExactCall
branch if condition eqExact target floorIntegral
call subOneCall math.subtractF64
argument subOneCall left F64 truncated
argument subOneCall right F64 oneFlr
run subOneCall
bind value floored CFloat64 subOneCall
return value floored
label floorIntegral
return value truncated

operation ceilingFloat64
input operation ceilingFloat64 inputValue CFloat64
output operation ceilingFloat64 CFloat64
memory ceilingFloat64 heap no
async ceilingFloat64 no
purpose operation ceilingFloat64 "Smallest integer >= x. Mirror image of floor: for x <= 0 trunc is the ceiling; for positive non-integer, ceil = trunc + 1."
label startCeilingFloat64
storage module immutable zeroCl CFloat64 0.0
storage module immutable oneCl CFloat64 1.0
call trcCall truncateFloat64TowardZero
argument trcCall x CFloat64 inputValue
run trcCall
bind ok cTruncated CFloat64 trcCall

call gtZeroCall math.greaterThanF64
argument gtZeroCall left CFloat64 inputValue
argument gtZeroCall right CFloat64 zeroCl
run gtZeroCall
bind value isPos Bool gtZeroCall
branch if condition isPos target ceilMaybeStepUp
return value cTruncated

label ceilMaybeStepUp
call eqExactCCall math.equalF64
argument eqExactCCall left F64 cTruncated
argument eqExactCCall right F64 inputValue
run eqExactCCall
bind value eqExactC Bool eqExactCCall
branch if condition eqExactC target ceilIntegral
call addOneCall math.addF64
argument addOneCall left F64 cTruncated
argument addOneCall right F64 oneCl
run addOneCall
bind value ceiled CFloat64 addOneCall
return value ceiled
label ceilIntegral
return value cTruncated

operation floatingRemainderFloat64
input operation floatingRemainderFloat64 dividendValue CFloat64
input operation floatingRemainderFloat64 divisorValue CFloat64
output operation floatingRemainderFloat64 CFloat64
memory floatingRemainderFloat64 heap no
async floatingRemainderFloat64 no
purpose operation floatingRemainderFloat64 "Floating-point remainder of x/y, truncating toward zero. fmod(x, y) = x - trunc(x/y) * y. Caller must ensure y != 0; we don't return NaN here (no IEEE special-value handling yet)."
label startFloatingRemainderFloat64
call qCall math.divideF64
argument qCall left F64 dividendValue
argument qCall right F64 divisorValue
run qCall
bind value floatRemainderQuotient CFloat64 qCall
call qTruncCall truncateFloat64TowardZero
argument qTruncCall x CFloat64 floatRemainderQuotient
run qTruncCall
bind ok qTrunc CFloat64 qTruncCall
call scaleCall math.multiplyF64
argument scaleCall left F64 qTrunc
argument scaleCall right F64 divisorValue
run scaleCall
bind value scaled CFloat64 scaleCall
call remCall math.subtractF64
argument remCall left F64 dividendValue
argument remCall right F64 scaled
run remCall
bind value rem CFloat64 remCall
return value rem

operation naturalLogFloat64
input operation naturalLogFloat64 inputValue CFloat64
output operation naturalLogFloat64 CFloat64
memory naturalLogFloat64 heap no
async naturalLogFloat64 no
purpose operation naturalLogFloat64 "Natural log of x for x > 0. Uses range reduction (ln(x) = ln(x / 2^k) + k * ln(2), where k is chosen so x/2^k is in [1, 2)) before Newton iteration on f(y) = exp(y) - x. exponentialBaseEFloat64 is accurate in [0, 2], so the reduced ln(scaled) computation stays in the convergent zone. Returns 0.0 for x <= 0."
invariant operation naturalLogFloat64 "Range-reduces x by repeated halving/doubling until in [1,2), then Newton-iterates on f(y) = exp(y) - x. Tracks the reduction factor k and re-applies via + k*ln(2) at the end."
label startNaturalLogFloat64
storage module immutable zeroLn CFloat64 0.0
storage module immutable oneLn CFloat64 1.0
storage module immutable twoLn CFloat64 2.0
storage module immutable halfLn CFloat64 0.5
storage module immutable ln2Const CFloat64 0.6931471805599453

call posCall math.lessThanOrEqualF64
argument posCall left CFloat64 inputValue
argument posCall right CFloat64 zeroLn
run posCall
bind value nonPos Bool posCall
branch if condition nonPos target lnReturnZero

# Range reduction: pull x into [1, 2) by halving / doubling, tracking
# the exponent shift k as an int.
memory naturalLogFloat64 mutable scaled CFloat64 0.0
set memory scaled inputValue
memory naturalLogFloat64 mutable kInt I64 0
storage module immutable oneIk I64 1
storage module immutable negOneIk I64 -1

label halveBigger
call gtTwoCall math.greaterThanOrEqualF64
argument gtTwoCall left CFloat64 scaled
argument gtTwoCall right CFloat64 twoLn
run gtTwoCall
bind value isBig Bool gtTwoCall
branch if condition isBig target halveOne
branch else target maybeDouble
label halveOne
call halveCall math.multiplyF64
argument halveCall left F64 scaled
argument halveCall right F64 halfLn
run halveCall
bind value halved CFloat64 halveCall
set memory scaled halved
call kIncCall math.addI64
argument kIncCall left I64 kInt
argument kIncCall right I64 oneIk
run kIncCall
bind value kNext I64 kIncCall
set memory kInt kNext
jump target halveBigger

label maybeDouble
call ltOneCall math.lessThanF64
argument ltOneCall left F64 scaled
argument ltOneCall right F64 oneLn
run ltOneCall
bind value isSmall Bool ltOneCall
branch if condition isSmall target doubleOne
branch else target lnNewton
label doubleOne
call dblCall math.multiplyF64
argument dblCall left F64 scaled
argument dblCall right F64 twoLn
run dblCall
bind value doubled CFloat64 dblCall
set memory scaled doubled
call kDecCall math.addI64
argument kDecCall left I64 kInt
argument kDecCall right I64 negOneIk
run kDecCall
bind value kPrev I64 kDecCall
set memory kInt kPrev
jump target maybeDouble

label lnNewton
# scaled is in [1, 2). Newton's on ln(scaled). Initial guess scaled - 1
# (in [0, 1)) gives fast convergence.
memory naturalLogFloat64 mutable y CFloat64 0.0
call initCall math.subtractF64
argument initCall left F64 scaled
argument initCall right F64 oneLn
run initCall
bind value y0 CFloat64 initCall
set memory y y0

memory naturalLogFloat64 mutable iterL I64 0
storage module immutable maxIterLn I64 25
storage module immutable oneIL I64 1

label lnIter
call expYCall exponentialBaseEFloat64
argument expYCall x CFloat64 y
run expYCall
bind ok eyVal CFloat64 expYCall
call resCall math.subtractF64
argument resCall left F64 eyVal
argument resCall right F64 scaled
run resCall
bind value newtonResidual CFloat64 resCall
call deltaCall math.divideF64
argument deltaCall left F64 newtonResidual
argument deltaCall right F64 eyVal
run deltaCall
bind value delta CFloat64 deltaCall
call newYCall math.subtractF64
argument newYCall left F64 y
argument newYCall right F64 delta
run newYCall
bind value newY CFloat64 newYCall
set memory y newY

call iterIncCall math.addI64
argument iterIncCall left I64 iterL
argument iterIncCall right I64 oneIL
run iterIncCall
bind value nextIter I64 iterIncCall
set memory iterL nextIter
call iterDoneCall math.greaterThanOrEqualI64
argument iterDoneCall left I64 iterL
argument iterDoneCall right I64 maxIterLn
run iterDoneCall
bind value done Bool iterDoneCall
branch if condition done target lnDone
branch else target lnIter

label lnDone
# Result = y + k * ln(2)
call kFloatCall math.intToFloat
argument kFloatCall value I64 kInt
run kFloatCall
bind value kFloat CFloat64 kFloatCall
call kLnCall math.multiplyF64
argument kLnCall left F64 kFloat
argument kLnCall right F64 ln2Const
run kLnCall
bind value kTimesLn CFloat64 kLnCall
call finalCall math.addF64
argument finalCall left F64 y
argument finalCall right F64 kTimesLn
run finalCall
bind value finalLn CFloat64 finalCall
return value finalLn

label lnReturnZero
return value zeroLn

operation powerFloat64
input operation powerFloat64 baseValue CFloat64
input operation powerFloat64 exponentValue CFloat64
output operation powerFloat64 CFloat64
memory powerFloat64 heap no
async powerFloat64 no
purpose operation powerFloat64 "base^exponent for base > 0 via exp(exponent * ln(base)). Returns 1.0 for exponent == 0."
label startPowerFloat64
storage module immutable zeroPw CFloat64 0.0
storage module immutable oneFw CFloat64 1.0
call eExpCall math.equalF64
argument eExpCall left F64 exponentValue
argument eExpCall right F64 zeroPw
run eExpCall
bind value expZero Bool eExpCall
branch if condition expZero target powOne
call lnBCall naturalLogFloat64
argument lnBCall x CFloat64 baseValue
run lnBCall
bind ok lnBase CFloat64 lnBCall
call prodCall math.multiplyF64
argument prodCall left F64 exponentValue
argument prodCall right F64 lnBase
run prodCall
bind value prod CFloat64 prodCall
call eResCall exponentialBaseEFloat64
argument eResCall x CFloat64 prod
run eResCall
bind ok powVal CFloat64 eResCall
return value powVal
label powOne
return value oneFw

operation sineRadiansFloat64
input operation sineRadiansFloat64 inputValue CFloat64
output operation sineRadiansFloat64 CFloat64
memory sineRadiansFloat64 heap no
async sineRadiansFloat64 no
purpose operation sineRadiansFloat64 "sin(x) via Taylor series: x - x^3/3! + x^5/5! - x^7/7! + ... (20 terms). For best accuracy, caller should reduce x into [-pi, pi] beforehand. No argument reduction at this layer (yet)."
invariant operation sineRadiansFloat64 "Sums 20 Taylor terms with alternating sign; each iteration multiplies the running term by -x^2 / ((2k)(2k+1))."
label startSineRadiansFloat64
storage module immutable oneISn I64 1
storage module immutable maxTermsSn I64 20

# accumulator = x (first term)
memory sineRadiansFloat64 mutable sumSn CFloat64 0.0
set memory sumSn inputValue
memory sineRadiansFloat64 mutable termSn CFloat64 0.0
set memory termSn inputValue
memory sineRadiansFloat64 mutable kSn I64 1
memory sineRadiansFloat64 mutable kFlSn CFloat64 1.0

label sinLoop
call sinDoneCall math.greaterThanOrEqualI64
argument sinDoneCall left I64 kSn
argument sinDoneCall right I64 maxTermsSn
run sinDoneCall
bind value doneSn Bool sinDoneCall
branch if condition doneSn target sinReturn

# Next term factor: -x^2 / ((2k)(2k+1)). We update term by multiplying.
# numerator: -x*x
call xSqCall math.multiplyF64
argument xSqCall left F64 inputValue
argument xSqCall right F64 inputValue
run xSqCall
bind value xSq CFloat64 xSqCall
storage module immutable negOneSn CFloat64 -1.0
call negXSqCall math.multiplyF64
argument negXSqCall left F64 xSq
argument negXSqCall right F64 negOneSn
run negXSqCall
bind value negXSq CFloat64 negXSqCall

# denominator: (2k)(2k+1) Ã¢â‚¬â€ both ints.
storage module immutable twoIsn I64 2
call twoKCall math.multiplyI64
argument twoKCall left I64 kSn
argument twoKCall right I64 twoIsn
run twoKCall
bind value twoK I64 twoKCall
call twoKPlus1Call math.addI64
argument twoKPlus1Call left I64 twoK
argument twoKPlus1Call right I64 oneISn
run twoKPlus1Call
bind value twoKplus I64 twoKPlus1Call
call denomIntCall math.multiplyI64
argument denomIntCall left I64 twoK
argument denomIntCall right I64 twoKplus
run denomIntCall
bind value denomInt I64 denomIntCall
call denomFloatCall math.intToFloat
argument denomFloatCall value I64 denomInt
run denomFloatCall
bind value denomFl CFloat64 denomFloatCall

# term *= negXSq / denomFl
call termMulCall math.multiplyF64
argument termMulCall left F64 termSn
argument termMulCall right F64 negXSq
run termMulCall
bind value tmpTerm CFloat64 termMulCall
call termDivCall math.divideF64
argument termDivCall left F64 tmpTerm
argument termDivCall right F64 denomFl
run termDivCall
bind value nextTerm CFloat64 termDivCall
set memory termSn nextTerm

# sum += term
call sumAddCall math.addF64
argument sumAddCall left F64 sumSn
argument sumAddCall right F64 nextTerm
run sumAddCall
bind value nextSum CFloat64 sumAddCall
set memory sumSn nextSum

call kSnIncCall math.addI64
argument kSnIncCall left I64 kSn
argument kSnIncCall right I64 oneISn
run kSnIncCall
bind value nextK I64 kSnIncCall
set memory kSn nextK
jump target sinLoop

label sinReturn
return value sumSn

operation cosineRadiansFloat64
input operation cosineRadiansFloat64 inputValue CFloat64
output operation cosineRadiansFloat64 CFloat64
memory cosineRadiansFloat64 heap no
async cosineRadiansFloat64 no
purpose operation cosineRadiansFloat64 "cos(x) via Taylor series: 1 - x^2/2! + x^4/4! - ... (20 terms). Same range caveat as sineRadiansFloat64."
invariant operation cosineRadiansFloat64 "Sums 20 Taylor terms starting from 1.0; each iteration multiplies the running term by -x^2 / ((2k-1)(2k))."
label startCosineRadiansFloat64
storage module immutable oneICs I64 1
storage module immutable maxTermsCs I64 20

memory cosineRadiansFloat64 mutable sumCs CFloat64 1.0
memory cosineRadiansFloat64 mutable termCs CFloat64 1.0
memory cosineRadiansFloat64 mutable kCs I64 1
memory cosineRadiansFloat64 mutable kFlCs CFloat64 1.0

label cosLoop
call csDoneCall math.greaterThanOrEqualI64
argument csDoneCall left I64 kCs
argument csDoneCall right I64 maxTermsCs
run csDoneCall
bind value doneCs Bool csDoneCall
branch if condition doneCs target cosReturn

# denominator: (2k-1)(2k)
storage module immutable twoICs I64 2
call twoKcCall math.multiplyI64
argument twoKcCall left I64 kCs
argument twoKcCall right I64 twoICs
run twoKcCall
bind value twoKc I64 twoKcCall
call twoKcMinus1Call math.subtractI64
argument twoKcMinus1Call left I64 twoKc
argument twoKcMinus1Call right I64 oneICs
run twoKcMinus1Call
bind value twoKcm1 I64 twoKcMinus1Call
call denomCsIntCall math.multiplyI64
argument denomCsIntCall left I64 twoKcm1
argument denomCsIntCall right I64 twoKc
run denomCsIntCall
bind value denomCsInt I64 denomCsIntCall
call denomCsFlCall math.intToFloat
argument denomCsFlCall value I64 denomCsInt
run denomCsFlCall
bind value denomCsFl CFloat64 denomCsFlCall

# numerator: -x*x
call xSqCsCall math.multiplyF64
argument xSqCsCall left F64 inputValue
argument xSqCsCall right F64 inputValue
run xSqCsCall
bind value xSqCs CFloat64 xSqCsCall
storage module immutable negOneCs CFloat64 -1.0
call negXSqCsCall math.multiplyF64
argument negXSqCsCall left F64 xSqCs
argument negXSqCsCall right F64 negOneCs
run negXSqCsCall
bind value negXSqCs CFloat64 negXSqCsCall

call termCsMulCall math.multiplyF64
argument termCsMulCall left F64 termCs
argument termCsMulCall right F64 negXSqCs
run termCsMulCall
bind value tmpTermCs CFloat64 termCsMulCall
call termCsDivCall math.divideF64
argument termCsDivCall left F64 tmpTermCs
argument termCsDivCall right F64 denomCsFl
run termCsDivCall
bind value nextTermCs CFloat64 termCsDivCall
set memory termCs nextTermCs

call sumCsAddCall math.addF64
argument sumCsAddCall left F64 sumCs
argument sumCsAddCall right F64 nextTermCs
run sumCsAddCall
bind value nextSumCs CFloat64 sumCsAddCall
set memory sumCs nextSumCs

call kCsIncCall math.addI64
argument kCsIncCall left I64 kCs
argument kCsIncCall right I64 oneICs
run kCsIncCall
bind value nextKcs I64 kCsIncCall
set memory kCs nextKcs
jump target cosLoop

label cosReturn
return value sumCs

operation tangentRadiansFloat64
input operation tangentRadiansFloat64 inputValue CFloat64
output operation tangentRadiansFloat64 CFloat64
memory tangentRadiansFloat64 heap no
async tangentRadiansFloat64 no
purpose operation tangentRadiansFloat64 "tan(x) = sin(x) / cos(x). Inherits range caveats from sineRadiansFloat64 / cosineRadiansFloat64 (no argument reduction)."
label startTangentRadiansFloat64
call sCall sineRadiansFloat64
argument sCall x CFloat64 inputValue
run sCall
bind ok sV CFloat64 sCall
